import { JsonReaderCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> JsonReaderCopy = JsonReaderCopy {
  summary: "Read JSON settings and check their fields and types before using them. This module also writes settings as JSON text and reads JSON without first choosing an application type.",
  taskTitle: "Read settings and use the checked name",
  purpose: "Suppose notification settings contain a name and a retry count. Before printing Ready: Mio, check not only that the text is valid JSON, but that name is text and retries is an integer. Display Settings rejected for invalid settings.",
  typescriptBefore: "TypeScript readSettings receives JSON.parse output as unknown and catches malformed JSON with try/catch. It then checks for an object rather than null or an array, rejects extra fields, checks name is a string, and checks retries is a safe integer. describe branches on the returned Settings or null, so it never reads a name from unchecked input.",
  benefit: "In Seseragi, adding deriving JsonDecode to the settings type asks the compiler to supply the field checks. The data declaration and its basic reading rules stay together, and the caller receives Settings only after those checks succeed. The TypeScript example also genuinely validates these inputs.",
  setup: "import * as json from \"std/json\" makes this module available under the name json. struct Settings defines a named data type; name: String and retries: Int give the field names and their types. deriving JsonDecode requests the checks needed to read this type from JSON.",
  after: [
    "fn readSettings source: String -> Either<json.JsonReadError, Settings> defines an ordinary function taking a string named source. The type after -> is its return type. Either holds one of two outcomes: here, Left carries a reading failure and Right carries checked Settings. This return annotation selects Settings as the type decodeString should produce.",
    "json.decodeString source reads the string as JSON and then checks the Settings shape. Function arguments follow the function name, separated by spaces. describe also takes a String and returns a String; match readSettings source examines the reading result.",
    "The Right settings -> branch of match names the successful value settings and chooses the expression that follows. settings.name accesses the checked name, and + joins strings. Left _ -> chooses the failure branch; _ means this example does not use the detailed reason.",
    "The first input prints Ready: Mio. The input with name set to false and the unfinished JSON both print Settings rejected. In the source, \\\" includes a JSON double quote inside a string.",
    "pub effect fn main is the entry point that displays results. Unit means it has no useful return value; with Console fails ConsoleError declares console use and possible output failure. do sequences the prints, and _ <- discards each println result before continuing. readSettings checks JSON; main handles display.",
  ],
  details: [
    "Reading this Settings requires both name and retries and rejects extra fields. Int is checked for an integral value in its safe range. These checks do not impose product rules such as a nonempty name or nonnegative retries. Check such conditions separately after reading succeeds.",
    "To write settings as text, add deriving JsonEncode to the type and use json.encodeString. Reading support, JsonDecode, and writing support, JsonEncode, can be selected independently.",
    "When you have not chosen an application type, json.parse converts text to a Json value and json.stringify writes it back. Successful parsing checks JSON syntax, not the Settings fields. For an existing Json, decodeJson checks an application type; encodeJson builds Json from an application value.",
    "To select only needed fields from a larger API response, start with field; use optionalField for a field that may be absent, or array for array elements. The operations in this example work on in-memory text. They do not read files or make API requests.",
  ],
}
